version: 2.4.4

## How changelog entries are written

Write this changelog for people who use `pave-init`. Start with user impact.
Put implementation details in an indented sub-bullet.

Start each top-level entry with one tag:

- New — a capability is available.
- Improved — existing behavior is more reliable or easier to use.
- Fixed — incorrect behavior now works.
- Changed — a default or contract changed.
- Updated — a dependency or runtime requirement changed.

Add each release at the top of `## Changelog`, newest first. Bump the `version:`
line and use the release date.

## Changelog

### 2.4.4 (2026-09-03)

Changed — a review of work a loop lands sits inside the loop, on a batch
cadence the planner declares and the lead sizes per batch by complexity,
recorded in run state; the whole-composition review at promotion is in
addition, not instead (`references/pave-spec.md` §9.4, §9.7). "One existing
gate" now means the same claim before the same harm — a gate after a loop is
not the gate for the laps inside it (§4.11); new smell "Review parked behind
a loop" (§13).
  - Field evidence: a generated workflow placed its only implementation
    review after its whole increment loop, citing §4.11's existing-gate rule
    at three sites in its planning record and defining harm as the hardware
    spend the review preceded; 55 increments (57 commits) landed with zero
    implementation review, each building on the last.
Changed — evolving tier: a run paused on a graph defect continues on the
reviewed, frozen successor from its last satisfied gate; a gate the
successor adds becomes a backfill duty for landed work; only a successor
that changes what a recorded outcome meant closes the run and starts a
linked one (`references/pave-revisions.md` rules 3, 7; tier item 1).
  - Field evidence: the same run needed one review node added mid-run; the
    contract's only path was close the run and start a successor, so the
    operator ordered the frozen graph edited in place instead.

### 2.4.3 (2026-09-02)

Changed — the instrument question is asked at every entry, not only on
re-entry: each node records, beside its sizing line, what settles it — a
lead-run mechanical check, a seat dispatched only when a named trigger
fires, or a full seat (`references/pave-spec.md` §2.1, §9.12). A node whose
common path any reader re-derives from persisted inputs is a lead-run check
whatever role its parent carries; tiers bind to roles, instruments bind to
nodes, and a child inherits roles, never a seat.
  - Field evidence: a generated workflow dispatched a strong-model seat 51
    times to pick the next work item from an on-disk gate table. The seat was
    inherited from the parent's role label and priced on a judgment that
    fired 0 of 51 laps; no layer asked whether the node needed a seat at all.
Changed — every dispatched seat joins the enforcement record (§9.14.1): the
defect it prices and that defect's expected frequency over the traversals
the node can carry. The simplicity pass (§4.11) prices a seat on a loop at
its declared traversal count, and removes a seat on a node a lead-run check
settles.
Changed — the material reviewer treats a seat on a node whose own sizing
line calls its outcome a derivation from persisted inputs as
disproportionate recurring cost at generation time, not only on re-entry;
the mechanical/judgment split rule now lives in §2.1 and the reviewer and
planner point at it.
Changed — generated leads settle any lead-run instrument themselves (the
role-work carve-out no longer says "re-check" only) and list every such node
in their own dispatch row; a record minted per traversal is a run-state
entry, never a file, unless it is world-produced evidence (§8.4).
Changed — evolving tier: the usage ledger records each seat's outcome
distribution, a seat whose priced judgment never fired is a
lead-run-instrument candidate for the successor, and a binding revision
scopes its nodes from recorded traversals, never from a premise about which
stage ran (`references/pave-revisions.md` rules 8–9).
Changed — a repair loop terminates on the world, not on rounds: a lap that
re-enters a design node with no new world-produced evidence is a paper lap
and a design loop bounds consecutive paper laps; repair-introduced is a
defect class counted from round one; a bookkeeping defect routes to a lead
edit, never the design route (`references/pave-spec.md` §9.8).
  - Field evidence: a generated workflow ran its plan drafter 49 laps
    (1,790 minutes). 35 of 49 laps were triggered by review paper, not world
    evidence; the target's real config first landed at revision 46; about
    37% of late material findings were on text the previous repair minted;
    every stop was the user's.
Changed — materiality for an intermediate artifact runs through its
consumer (would the consuming node act wrongly on it as written, or a
frozen value move), a repair round reviews the briefed sections plus one
scripted census, and a design node names which inputs are world artifacts
and which are premises (§9.4, §9.2).
Changed — generated run state stamps every entry from one clock, the
validator flags an out-of-order append, and a dispatched seat's entry
carries model, effort, and duration so the usage ledger can price it
(`references/pave-revisions.md` rule 8 records wall-clock).
  - pave-init's own run state keeps its unconstrained `at` field: it runs
    no usage ledger, so the omission is recorded here, not fixed.

### 2.4.2 (2026-09-02)

Changed — every living document declares a cap (default 400 lines and 60 KB,
both) and the rules that keep it: landed work collapses to one ledger row,
count tables are script output, repair briefs are section-scoped, the
reviewer reports lines and bytes every round, and over cap means a deletion
lap before the next review lap (`references/pave-spec.md` §8.4). Generated
leads carry the cap and its instrument.
  - Field evidence: one generated workflow's living plan grew to 2,455 lines
    and 872 KB over 46 review laps because nothing bounded it; one repair lap
    then cost half an hour of re-reading. Fixes future generated plugins;
    an already-generated plugin needs its own patch release.
New — `scripts/measure_artifact.py`, the one size instrument: lines, bytes,
sections, narration markers, `--baseline` delta, `--strict` exit. Copied into
a generated plugin whose layout reference declares a capped living document.
Improved — `write_for_reader.sh` names an over-cap document with its size once
per session and file, past its throttle (`PAVE_INIT_CAP_LINES`,
`PAVE_INIT_CAP_BYTES`).
Improved — pave-init keeps its own budget: `tests/test_doc_budget.py` pins a
line and byte ceiling per document (raise a ceiling only in the same commit
that needs it), CI runs the pave-init test suite, and the §8.4 restatements
in the lead skill, orchestration, and role contracts — plus the lead skill's
§8.5 restatement — are now pointers.
Fixed — the README file tree lists `write_for_reader.sh` and every test.

### 2.4.1 (2026-09-02)

Fixed — the two hooks that watch subagent writes now fire on Claude Code.
`planning-layout-warn.sh` and `write_for_reader.sh` moved from the skill's
frontmatter to the plugin's `hooks/hooks.json`. Field evidence: a fresh
session with a logging hook in each placement (run twice; the second run's
logs are kept) — the skill-frontmatter hook logged only the lead's write; the
settings-level hook logged that write plus both subagents', with `agent_id`
and `agent_type` in the payload; a hook in an agent's own frontmatter logged
nothing. With this
fix loaded as a plugin, the reader hook's throttle counter reached 2 for one
lead write plus one subagent write inside a marked run. The layout warning's
main target is a node planner's write, so in 2.4.0 it could never fire for
the actor it names.

- `references/lead-alignment-hooks.md` (Scoping, Placement, Registration)
  and the Stage 5 hook bindings state the rule: register by the actor the
  hook watches — skill frontmatter for lead-only hooks, the plugin's
  `hooks/hooks.json` for hooks that must see subagent tool calls. The stop
  check and the staleness reminder stay in the frontmatter (lead-only by
  design). `tests/test_hooks.sh` checks the registration.
- Plugin-level placement is wider than the invoking session: any session in
  the repository whose `.pave-init-run` marker points at a live run state is
  in scope. Both moved hooks therefore also go silent once the run records a
  terminal status, like the stop check and the staleness reminder, so the
  marker-retaining abandon path silences all four.
- A generated plugin that followed the old guidance carries the same blind
  spot for any hook aimed at a subagent. vllm-neuron-parity is unaffected;
  its hooks were already plugin-level.

Fixed — the Codex binding names the agent tools Codex actually exposes.
`followup_task` continues a retained reviewer thread, `wait_agent` waits, and
`interrupt_agent` stops a thread that still runs after its node closed; the
nonexistent `send_input` and "close each thread" instructions are gone.
Dispatch policy is unchanged. Field evidence: the maintainer's Codex rollout
logs hold 1,090 `followup_task` calls and zero `send_input` calls.

- `sources/bindings/codex.toml`, `codex/README.md`; `scripts/build_packages.py`
  now also refuses `followup_task`, `wait_agent`, `interrupt_agent`, and
  `fork_turns` in shared sources.
- Known limit: `codex/preflight.py` still proves only the nested spawn chain,
  and its rollout-shape expectations (`agent_id` in the spawn output,
  `agent_role` in session metadata) match none of the rollouts observed on the
  maintainer's machine. Reviewer continuation is not proven by the preflight.

Fixed — release stamping works with a multi-plugin marketplace.
`scripts/stamp_version.py` stamps only the marketplace entry named after the
pave-init manifest and fails clearly when that entry is missing or listed
twice. The marketplace pin and the README version, stale at 2.2.9 since a
generated plugin joined the marketplace, are current again.

Improved — the lead applies three of its own generated-workflow rules to its
own run. Field evidence: a real run re-dispatched xhigh planners on unchanged
inputs and saw one defect class recur across four plan-review rounds.

- Dispatch rule on re-entry (`SKILL.md`, after the recovery table): a
  re-entered node whose inputs did not move is settled by the lead with the
  check's command and output recorded; a planner, explorer, or reviewer is
  re-dispatched only when an input to its judgment changed.
- Default recovery for its own failures: validation, build, forward-test, and
  preflight failures the graph does not route enter the §9.8 loop; the
  `build_failure_retry` and `delivery_retry` self-loops are one retry each.
- Plan review is bounded at four total rounds and counts recurrence by defect
  class: a class repeating at a new site widens the repair to a sweep; a third
  occurrence routes `repair_blocked`.

Improved — pave-init's own run-state validator is honest without jsonschema.
The stdlib path now enforces `minLength`, `const`, `enum`, and nested
`required` (an empty `run_id` fails in both modes) and derives the list of
keywords it cannot enforce from the schema instead of a typed string.
`traversal_history[].outcome`, `terminal_classification.status`, and the
`fitness_override` record's free text gain `maxLength`.

- Changed — in run-state mode, `maxLength` overflows are warnings with or
  without jsonschema (observation over blocking, §9.14.2); in 2.4.0 they were
  errors. A delivered run's state that exited 1 on caps now passes with
  warnings. Frontier mode keeps per-entry caps as errors (§8.1).
- `skills/pave-init/tests/test_validate_run_state.py` (new, stdlib only) runs
  under both interpreters.

Fixed — pave-init's own graph names the evidence paths the run writes:
`planning/root.draft.pave.yaml`, `planning/<node>.draft.pave.yaml`,
`run-state.json` `boundary_review_results` (no per-boundary review file), and
`build/`; the traceability rows match. Found by an independent Codex review.

Fixed — pave-init's own graph now routes Stage 3 the way the procedure runs
it, so a resume that consults the graph sees the queue-growth step. The lead
freezes the root contract and opens the queue with the root as its first
entry; every entry — the root included — is dispatched, reviewed, and on a
pass widens the queue with its framed children, at any depth. Before, the
graph planned the root in its own node with no boundary review, sent every
passed boundary straight to the join, and named no node that grew the queue.
No mechanism consumed the old routes (resume reads `frontier.yaml`), so this
is a fidelity fix, not a behavior change. Found by the same Codex review.

- `references/pave-init.pave.yaml`: `plan_root_skeleton` is lead-only and
  produces `root_contract` (`planning/root-contract.md`; formerly
  `root_skeleton` at the planner's draft path) plus the opened frontier;
  `review_boundary` gains the lead half and `frontier_record` as a product;
  `frontier_record`, the fan-out edge, and the frontier join describe a
  growing, never-empty queue. Node, outcome, and edge ids are unchanged;
  `pave-init-traceability.md` rows and the README wording follow. `SKILL.md`
  Stage 4 drops the dead "empty frontier" clause.

Changed — two rules moved from `technique-selection.md`, which loads only for
supervision choices, into `pave-spec.md`, which every design choice loads:
"an authored mechanism, not a knob flip" (§9.1) and "scripts own derived
numbers" (§5.3.1). Net +12 spec lines; no restatement existed to cut.

### 2.4.0 (2026-09-01)

Improved — generated workflows carry a document budget: a run keeps a few
bounded standing documents instead of minting a file per event, and evidence
is written at the moment of world contact. Field evidence: a generated run
accumulated dozens of per-event files and batched transcripts that one
compaction could lose.

- `references/pave-spec.md` §8.4: three standing docs by mutability plus run
  state; events append, never mint; superseded content is deleted, not
  archived; cite, never copy. Carve-outs: world-produced per-event evidence
  lands at the event (applied to every world-contact node or none), and
  collision-safety working state stays out of the budget.

Improved — ten efficiency rules mined from a full production run (GLM 5.3
Flash port) now shape generated plans, so future runs spend less on waste the
graph can screen out cheaply.

- Dispatch admission (`pave-spec.md` §9.2): a plan node feeding an expensive
  execute node discharges the cheap half of the acceptance pre-dispatch —
  names resolve, products exist, surfaces owned — starting as an observing
  count, promoted to routing only on a real run's evidence.
- Briefs are rendered views (`SKILL.md`, `references/approval-briefs.md`): a
  dispatch brief cites evidence keys and resolved paths, never retypes facts;
  the artifact wins on disagreement.
- Repair recurrence counts by defect class, not site (`pave-spec.md` §9.8):
  the second occurrence of a class widens the repair to a sweep; the third
  routes to the declared stop.
- Standing approvals latch (`pave-spec.md` §8.3): a re-entered gate recomputes
  its frozen inputs and passes when nothing moved; `authority: user` evidence
  is recorded once, never re-asked by shape.
- Run-state hygiene (`schemas/run-state.schema.json`,
  `scripts/validate_run_state.py`): per-entry free-text fields carry
  maxLength caps with one declared escape hatch and a whole-file warn
  threshold; the stdlib fallback enforces the caps itself and names what it
  cannot enforce; recorded paths that resolve nowhere warn; question entries
  are pruned only after the ruling is verified at its pointer.
- Capture discipline (`pave-spec.md` §5.2/§9.10): evidence records the
  immutable identifier actually read (commit, digest, etag) — never `HEAD` or
  a branch name; measuring instruments register once by path and digest.
- Reviewer routing (`sources/roles/pave-material-reviewer.md.tmpl`): the gate
  reviewer now flags site-keyed recurrence stops, missing dispatch-admission
  duties, re-asked approvals, moving-ref evidence, and unregistered
  instruments as findings.
- Optional dispatch-advisory hook (`references/lead-alignment-hooks.md`): an
  advisory PreToolUse template that fires only on re-entry dispatches, asking
  whether the seat's question is already settled and whether the brief cites
  rather than retypes. Plan-time adoption only, never a default registration.

New — documents are written for the reader, and a hook keeps it that way.
Field evidence: a production run's design record compressed events into
identifier chains and inlined checker output that no person could parse.

- `references/pave-spec.md` §8.5 "Written for the reader": every entry a
  person will read leads with one plain-english sentence, pairs each id with
  its plain name at first use, and keeps digests and checker output in run
  state or logs — the test is a stranger parsing the entry in one read.
  Working state written only for the next agent is exempt.
- The reviewer treats an illegible reader-facing entry as a **material**
  finding (`sources/roles/pave-material-reviewer.md.tmpl`); writer roles and
  the lead carry the duty in their contracts.
- New registered hook `hooks/write_for_reader.sh` (fourth disclosed hook,
  both harnesses): a throttled PostToolUse advisory that fires when any
  actor — lead or subagent — writes a run-workspace document, re-injecting
  the duty at the moment it binds. Generated workflows ship the same
  reminder by default with a recorded omission condition (Stage 3/Stage 5).

Changed — the spec pays for its own growth: this release's additions to
`pave-spec.md` are offset by deleting restatement, so the document planners
read every dispatch is no larger than before.

- Removed §15 Summary and §16 Version history (git and this changelog own
  history; the summary restated every section) and condensed the AMMO worked
  example's diagram/roles/enforcement subsections into §12.3 prose. No
  doctrine changed; zero external citations pointed at the removed text.

### 2.3.0 (2026-08-28)

Improved — plans now price repair loops and re-entries, not just the first
pass. Field evidence: a generated run re-dispatched full-strength seats on
repair laps whose inputs had not changed.

- `references/pave-spec.md`: §4.11 prices added structure over the
  traversals its loops and re-entry edges can carry; §2.1 adds the re-entry
  instrument as a Runtime Binding question; §9.8 repair records state the
  evidence the change did **not** invalidate (unchanged verified facts settle
  mechanically, changed inputs to judgment get fresh eyes) and repair edges
  land at the resolving node, not the boundary entrance.
- `sources/roles/node-planner.md.tmpl`: a node any re-entry edge targets
  states its re-entry instrument, so re-entries are never silently priced at
  first-entry effort.
- `sources/pave-init/SKILL.md.tmpl` Stage 5: generated leads carry the
  dispatch rule — traversal is mandatory, dispatch is not; mechanically
  knowable re-checks settle lead-side with the check's command and output
  recorded, carved out of the role-work prohibition.

Improved — the enforcement record is two-sided: a rung with standing cost
also records why a cheaper rung is insufficient, changed in lockstep at
§9.14.1 and its five restatement sites.

- Reviewer contract: disproportionate recurring cost is material and can
  reach `HIGH`; worth is judged per check (a check bundling a mechanical
  half with a judgment half gets split, never averaged); planner-return
  checklist asks the delta question of every re-entry edge.

Improved — the clean-room forward test prefers a prompt that forces at
least one bounded repair edge; when none is affordable, loop traffic is
recorded as untested, never a silent clean pass.

Fixed — `references/lead-alignment-hooks.md` named PreCompact as a
reinjection channel; PreCompact cannot inject (its output is discarded).
Reinjection now names SessionStart's compact matcher, the payload carries
standing user directives, and the ~5k-token per-skill re-injection cap is
stated so oversized leads know their tail decays.

New — the evolving-tier contract gains two rules in
`references/pave-revisions.md`: rule 8 (usage ledger — the lead derives a
per-boundary usage record at run close from the existing event history;
successor drafts must read predecessors' records and their semantic diff
must engage them; actuals exceeding declared expectations are a
close-time successor trigger, never a mid-run gate) and rule 9 (binding
revisions — seat, model, effort, or re-entry instrument changes ship as a
user-approved, append-only `binding-revisions.yaml` record without a graph
successor; same check, cheaper instrument, mechanical evidence still
produced is not a weakening of check strength).

- Companions: rule 6's "(never weaker)" points at rule 9's bound;
  `binding-revisions.yaml` added to the workspace structure; the
  count-coupled "seven-rule" phrasing in the builder and Stage 5 texts
  replaced with count-free wording.

New — latent-rule reminder guidance in `lead-alignment-hooks.md`: target
reminder machinery by latent-rule count times blast radius, never wall-clock
length, with two derived plan-time options — an advisory dispatch-time check
edge-triggered on re-entry dispatches, and tool-class latent-rule
reinjection for rule-heavy seats (run-wide with a recorded degradation where
seat identity cannot be gated).

### 2.2.9 (2026-08-26)

Improved — an evolving-tier successor revision must justify itself as a
general fix, not an incident patch. The semantic diff's why now names
the gap the predecessor's plan missed and states why the change holds
for runs in general; a reason that cannot be stated apart from the
triggering run is a review finding.

- `references/pave-revisions.md` evolution contract rule 4: a successor
  ships to every future run, so a fix shaped to one run's particulars
  degrades all the others. The existing independent-review gate (rule 5)
  enforces the claim — unfalsifiable reasons were already findings.

### 2.2.8 (2026-08-24)

Improved — the default recovery loop opens investigation with cheap
priors, and exhaustion is judged over evidence channels, not source
lists. Driven by a live generated-campaign incident: 34 triage
iterations exhausted eleven on-disk sources while the answer sat in a
public issue tracker, because three individually correct rules —
unverified claims are non-evidence, workers never improvise beyond
grant, cite the record — composed into "never consult external sources
at all." The acceptance bar (§5.3.1) binds acceptance, never
investigation inputs; the generated culture had over-applied it.

- `references/pave-spec.md` §9.8 investigation step: open with
  documentation, release notes, issue trackers, and the failure text
  searched in public sources — priors that settle nothing but direct
  which expensive measurement runs first.
- §9.9 genuine pivot: a source list exhausted is not an investigation
  exhausted while an evidence channel stands untried.
- The same fix is applied to the live vllm-neuron-model-port skill
  (its 1.0.3) so the running campaign inherits it without waiting for
  a regeneration.

### 2.2.7 (2026-08-24)

- Fixed — shared-package generation now rejects malformed lead placeholders,
  quotes generated Markdown frontmatter safely, detects only real generated
  orphans across the full file, reports invalid UTF-8 without a traceback, and
  rolls back all outputs when an atomic replacement fails.
- Fixed — Codex now uses the native `$pave-init:pave-init` skill name and
  `pave-init:<role>` custom-agent names. The lead must run the live nested-agent
  preflight before Stage 1.
- Fixed — skill-quality validation now binds to the active harness:
  `skill-creator:skill-creator` for Claude Code and `$skill-creator` for Codex.
- Improved — CI parses generated Markdown frontmatter, validates binding schema
  values, checks placeholder and orphan edge cases, and tests atomic rollback.
- Known limit — the installed 2.2.7 plugin and namespaced roles load on Codex
  CLI 0.149.0, but the tested Bedrock runtime still rejects the first named V1
  spawn with `Invalid 'input': value did not match any expected variant`.

### 2.2.6 (2026-08-21)

Codex port follow-up — the native roles now preserve the Claude role model and
effort intent: `fable` and `opus` map to `gpt-5.6-sol`, `sonnet` maps to
`gpt-5.6-terra`, and each role keeps its approved effort. Codex V1 requires
`agents.max_depth = 2` for the reviewer and planner research delegates.

- Added a fail-closed release preflight that verifies the current generated
  source hash and the persisted root → reviewer → research-delegate thread
  chain. Model-written success prose cannot satisfy the gate.
- Added deterministic release stamping from this file to both plugin manifests,
  the marketplace entry, and the delivered README.
- Hardened shared-source generation: role templates reject every unresolved
  placeholder; normal builds refuse to overwrite a differing generated file;
  all 14 outputs carry `DO NOT EDIT`; CI checks drift, tests, stamps, and hooks.
- Restored doctrine lost during harness neutralization: concrete model mappings,
  reviewer continuity, explicit approval semantics, observing-first enforcement,
  generated-role and hook disclosure, stop-block cadence, and project-root
  discovery in the hook template.
- Codex CLI 0.149.0 still fails the first named V1 child request on the tested
  Bedrock runtime with `Invalid 'input': value did not match any expected
  variant`. The preflight records this as a release failure.

Improved — node planning and plan review now state what a decomposition
*is* before working with one: it rewrites the parent's definition of done
as an AND (all children) or OR (alternative children) of smaller
evidence-gated predicates, each settleable by world-produced evidence,
checked in both directions — a missing conjunct lets every child pass
while the parent fails; an unneeded child is inflation (§4.11).

- `agents/node-planner.md` step 5 opens with the framing (the planner's
  self-check while framing children); the completeness attack lands in
  `agents/pave-material-reviewer.md`'s "Is every element earned?" bullet
  (the independent detection): construct the run where every child
  succeeds while the parent's definition of done still fails.
- Placement was adversarially swept: 13 other candidate sites rejected
  as duplication under the skill's own doctrine (§4.7 reinjection at the
  binding moment, §4.11 smallest sufficient, §9.14.2 no always-on text
  for a rule one transition evaluates).
- A blind A/B review (planted-defect and clean fixtures, old vs new
  prompts) judged the change mixed → keep with revision. Planner half
  helps: the new-prompt plan covered a definition-of-done conjunct the
  old plan chartered no child to achieve, and was the less bloated of
  the pair. Reviewer detection was already at ceiling (both arms found
  the planted missing conjunct 2/2, sole BLOCKING). One probe-exhibited
  harm — a manufactured BLOCKING-shaped coverage finding on a clean
  plan — plus two doctrine gaps drove the two hardenings below.
- Hardenings: the entailment boundary is now one general rule — only
  the parent's definition of done decides what the entailment covers,
  and each clause needs a child that settles it, whether by build,
  disproof, or justified stop (§9.11); routing, ordering, and recovery
  that no clause names are machinery, and a gap there is a different
  finding. The reviewer's missing-conjunct attack requires quoting the
  unsettled clause and naming the short-stopping child — no findings
  built from actors the plan does not charter.
  - Replaces an earlier exception-shaped fix (special cases for
    exhaustion and disproof children); the general rule makes both
    cases fall out and stays scenario-free.
- Recorded, not done: a clean-fixture re-probe (4 reps per arm, scoring
  HIGH-severity inventions) — both arms currently over-fire on clean
  plans, a pre-existing calibration issue; and a PAVE 0.4 candidate
  paragraph for `references/pave-spec.md` §9.12 stating the entailment
  meaning of "child results integrate upward", a deliberate spec
  revision left for its own release.

### 2.2.5 (2026-08-19)

Fixed — the model/effort doctrine is consistent across planner, builder,
and assembly prose. A whole-skill consistency review found four conflicts
left by the 2.2.4 half-migration:

- The effort guidance no longer claims "unset means high": an unset
  `effort` inherits the session's effort — the same hazard as an unset
  model — so generated roles pin effort in frontmatter, with `high` as
  the default pin.
- Assignments are stated at the granularity the runtime can express:
  per-role frontmatter pins for lead-bound dispatches (the Agent tool
  has no per-dispatch effort override), per-`agent()` values inside
  workflow scripts. A role that needs two tiers on different nodes is
  two roles.
- The verify-retry escalation rule (a check that fails twice retries
  once one tier up) is scoped to workflow-script compilation, the only
  binding that can express it; the assembly step no longer states it as
  a graph-wide duty.
- The material reviewer no longer overrides `model` when dispatching
  research delegates (frontmatter pins are the authority), and the
  research delegate pins `effort: high` — every agent is now pinned
  except the deliberately-unpinned forward tester, as SKILL.md claims.

### 2.2.4 (2026-08-19)

Fixed — SKILL.md's Multi-agent contract no longer lists stale per-agent
model/effort pins or a per-dispatch planner escalation rule; both
contradicted the agent frontmatter, which has pinned the planner at the
