### 1.2.0 (2026-08-14)

Three change sets in one release: a node-sizing doctrine revision (from a port
run that mirrored source complexity), the promotion of `references/pave-spec.md`
to the single design-language home, and a structural verbosity reduction across
the package. No graph-topology, hook, script, schema, or test change.

Doctrine changes (all now homed in `references/pave-spec.md`, PAVE 0.3):

- Changed — node sizing is now the **one-agent test, applied recursively**:
  can one agent understand, achieve, and verify this purpose in one bounded
  context? Yes — atomic. No — decompose into independently verifiable
  subgoals and re-apply the test to each; child results integrate upward and
  are verified against the parent purpose. "The burden of proof is on
  decomposing" is retired; both wrong-sizings carry equal weight, and the
  realization ladder now chooses only the *form* of a justified
  decomposition. The depth-2 composition cap stays; its rationale now names
  the test as the real stopping rule.
  - `references/pave-spec.md` §9.12, `agents/node-planner.md`,
    SKILL.md Stage 3, `references/pave-composition.md` §6.5.
- New — **existing systems are evidence, not blueprints**: a port,
  migration, or re-design binds the source's behavior (acceptance,
  invariants, failure modes), never its topology; structure is re-derived
  from the goal with the one-agent test. Reviewer teeth: a child profile
  whose only support is "the source has this boundary" is a finding.
  - `references/pave-spec.md` §9.12.2, `agents/node-planner.md`,
    `agents/material-reviewer.md`, `orchestration/explore-and-plan.md`
    §4.1, SKILL.md Stage 3.
- Changed — root planners receive **`references/workflow-minimal.pave.yaml`**
  (new: the validating floor, 1 role / 1 node / 1 outcome / 1 edge /
  1 terminal) as the default starting graph.
  `workflow-template.pave.yaml` is re-labeled a construct showcase, sent
  only when the frozen root contract already names branching, recovery, or
  fan-out.
  - `orchestration/explore-and-plan.md` §4.2; header comments in both
    template files; README file tree.
- Changed — `atomic` verdicts are now falsifiable claims, symmetric with
  decompositions: every elaboration mark records `verdict` plus a
  one-line `justification` (what one agent does, how it verifies) citing
  system-map evidence. An atomic mark the evidence does not support is a
  review finding.
  - `agents/node-planner.md` (sizing rule and output contract),
    `agents/material-reviewer.md`, `orchestration/explore-and-plan.md`
    §4.8, `references/pave-spec.md` §9.12.
- Changed — the one-agent test is answered by writing the node contract and
  checking five conditions (one definition of done, enumerable inputs, local
  verification, routine steps, bounded context), not by intuition. A contract
  field that can only be filled vaguely is the decomposition signal; the
  `atomic` justification answers conditions 2–4 in one line. "One definition of
  done" permits a conjunction — faster AND still correct — when every part is
  settled by the same act on the node's own evidence; it splits when one
  criterion gates another or they fail to different recovery routes.
  - `references/pave-spec.md` §9.12,
    `agents/material-reviewer.md`, `agents/node-planner.md`.
- Changed — the **Node definition** now states what a node is: one goal plus
  the smallest contract that lets one actor achieve it and prove it. Five named
  parts (purpose, inputs, effects, outcomes, roles), a worked example carrying
  all of them, and the rule that the contract is the sizing
  artifact. The old definition — "one coherent unit of work" with six loose
  questions and an example showing neither a standard of done nor how the node
  verifies — is what let node sizing rest on taste.
  - `references/pave-spec.md` §5.1 and the §11 node card.
- Changed — the contract condensed from nine parts to five. The standard of
  done, its verification, and the success outcome were one fact written three
  times; they merge into a **definition of done** carried by the outcome that
  means success, written *condition — settled by act on world-produced
  evidence* — an unverifiable acceptance condition is now unwritable rather
  than merely discouraged. `Produces` folded into Effects (creating an
  artifact is an effect), and intent demoted to a label read from the purpose.
  A node run ends by reporting exactly one declared outcome, and only the
  settling act selects success. YAML fields are unchanged: `intent` stays
  required, `produces`, `allowed_effects`, and `forbidden_effects` stay
  separate. No schema or validator change.
  - `references/pave-spec.md` §5.1, §5.2 rule 3, §9.12 conditions 1 and 3,
    §11 node card; `agents/material-reviewer.md`.
- Changed — the definition of done names its evidence source, not just its
  check. Every definition of done is acceptance-bearing, so the acceptance
  ladder binds it — a condition whose only evidence is the doer's report that
  it finished is a finding. Naming an act was not enough if the act only read
  the actor's own claim. §5.3.1's exemption now names what it covers
  (control-flow outcomes) instead of reading as exempting node success. The
  actor settles the verdict; the persisted evidence lets a reviewer re-run the
  act and re-settle it — a definition of done a reviewer cannot re-settle from
  the artifacts is not verifiable, and the guard on the success edge is that
  re-settling built into the graph.
  - `references/pave-spec.md` §5.1, §5.3.1, §11 node card;
    `agents/node-planner.md`, `agents/material-reviewer.md`.
- New — acceptance evidence ladder: evidence for an acceptance-bearing
  outcome is produced by the world, never asserted by the doer. Rung 1 is a
  measurement against a declared threshold; rung 2 is LLM-as-judge, only for
  qualitative properties and only with a plan-time rubric, a non-doer judge,
  and persisted artifacts. Doer self-report is never sole acceptance
  evidence; an invented metric is a finding; control-flow outcomes stay
  evidence-free.
  - `references/pave-spec.md` §5.3.1, new Acceptance evidence section in
    `agents/material-reviewer.md`.
- Fixed — the planning frontier can no longer lose a node in transit:
